g line-type attribute is not dashed."}]}],"name":"dashLength"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The length, in tenths, of the spaces in a dashed line. Ignored if the corresponding line-type attribute is not dashed."}]}],"name":"spaceLength"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For most elements, any program will compute a default x and y position. The position attributes let this be changed two ways."}]}],"name":"position"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The placement attribute indicates whether something is above or below another element, such as a note or a notation."}]}],"name":"placement"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The orientation attribute indicates whether slurs and ties are overhand (tips down) or underhand (tips up). This is distinct from the placement attribute used by any notation type."}]}],"name":"orientation"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The x-coordinate, in tenths relative to any position settings, of the outgoing bezier control point for a slur or tie with a start type, or the incoming control point for one with a stop or continue type."}]}],"name":"bezierX"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The y-coordinate, in tenths relative to any position settings, of the outgoing bezier control point for a slur or tie with a start type, or the incoming control point for one with a stop or continue type."}]}],"name":"bezierY"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The x-coordinate, in tenths, of the outgoing bezier control point for a slur with a continue type."}]}],"name":"bezierX2"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The y-coordinate, in tenths, of the outgoing bezier control point for a slur with a continue type."}]}],"name":"bezierY2"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The outgoing bezier control point offset, in musical divisions, for a slur or tie with a start type, or the incoming control point offset for one with a stop or continue type. Deprecated as of MusicXML 3.1 in favor of "},{"code":"bezierX","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}],"name":"bezierOffset"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The outgoing bezier control point offset, in musical divisions, for a slur with a continue type. Deprecated as of MusicXML 3.1 in favor of "},{"code":"bezierX2","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}],"name":"bezierOffset2"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The color attribute group indicates the color of an element."}]}],"name":"color"}]}],"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLDivisions":{"abstract":[{"text":"The divisions type is used to express values in terms of the musical divisions defined by the divisions","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"element. It is preferred that these be integer values both for MIDI interoperability and to avoid roundoff","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"errors.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLDivisions","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLDivisions"}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxldivisions","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLDivisions","title":"MXLDivisions"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLLineKind":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLLineKind","type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLLineKind"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The line-type type distinguishes between solid, dashed, dotted, and wavy lines."}],"kind":"symbol","title":"MXLLineKind","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxllinekind","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLLineKind"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}],"title":"IvorMXL","kind":"symbol","role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLAboveBelow":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"The above-below type is used to indicate whether one element appears above or below another element.","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLAboveBelow"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLAboveBelow"}],"title":"MXLAboveBelow","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlabovebelow","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLAboveBelow"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLOverUnder":{"fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLOverUnder"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLOverUnder","title":"MXLOverUnder","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLOverUnder"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The over-under type is used to indicate whether the tips of curved lines such as slurs and ties are overhand"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(tips down) or underhand (tips up)."}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxloverunder"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLSlur":{"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","title":"MXLSlur","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlslur","abstract":[{"text":"Slur types are empty. Most slurs are represented with two elements: one with a start type, and one with a stop","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"type. Slurs can add more elements using a continue type. This is typically used to specify the formatting of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"cross-system slurs, or to specify the shape of very complex slurs.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLSlur"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSlur","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLSlur","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNumberLevel":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel","type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLNumberLevel"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Slurs, tuplets, and many other features can be concurrent and overlap within a single musical part. The"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"number-level entity distinguishes up to 16 concurrent objects of the same type when the objects overlap in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"MusicXML document order. Values greater than 6 are usually only needed for music with a large number of divisi"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"staves in a single part, or if there are more than 6 cross-staff arpeggios in a single measure. When a number-"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"level value is implied, the value is 1 by default."}],"kind":"symbol","title":"MXLNumberLevel","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnumberlevel","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLNumberLevel"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLColor":{"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLColor","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLColor","title":"MXLColor","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLColor"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The color type indicates the color of an element. Color may be represented as hexadecimal RGB triples, as in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"HTML, or as hexadecimal ARGB tuples, with the A indicating alpha of transparency. An alpha value of 00 is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"totally transparent; FF is totally opaque. If RGB is used, the A value is assumed to be FF.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlcolor"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLPosition":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLPosition","type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLPosition"}],"abstract":[{"text":"For most elements, any program will compute a default x and y position. The position attributes let this be","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"changed two ways.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","title":"MXLPosition","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlposition","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLPosition"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLSlur/init(id:kind:number:lineKind:dashLength:spaceLength:position:placement:orientation:bezierX:bezierY:bezierX2:bezierY2:bezierOffset:bezierOffset2:color:)":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSlur\/init(id:kind:number:lineKind:dashLength:spaceLength:position:placement:orientation:bezierX:bezierY:bezierX2:bezierY2:bezierOffset:bezierOffset2:color:)","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","title":"init(id:kind:number:lineKind:dashLength:spaceLength:position:placement:orientation:bezierX:bezierY:bezierX2:bezierY2:bezierOffset:bezierOffset2:color:)","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Creates a new "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"MXLSlur"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlslur\/init(id:kind:number:linekind:dashlength:spacelength:position:placement:orie